    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d138">

                <pb facs="0228" n="158" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv01/pg158"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">
                    Edward M. House Papers: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Special Representative</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >House</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to President Wilson
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi>
                        </placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1918-11-10">November 10,
                            1918</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>10. In view of the Republican resentment of Governor
                    McCall’s attitude in the recent elections and in
                    anticipation of a hostile Senate, would it not be well to increase the
                    membership of the delegates to seven and include another Republican of the
                    standing and influence of Root?<anchor n="3" xml:id="d138-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>In the event that for any reason you think it best not to name any member of the
                    Cabinet other than Lansing, may I suggest
                        Sharp as a desirable delegate.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Edward House</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
